0" fill="#FFFFFF" text-anchor="middle">5</text><text x="748" y="637" font-size="21" font-weight="700" fill="#342E2A">Heavy items low</text><text x="748" y="665" font-size="16" fill="#6E645C">Big books and baskets on bottom shelves.</text><rect x="60" y="730" width="1080" height="100" rx="14" fill="#DCE5D6" stroke="#8FA689" stroke-width="2"/><rect x="60" y="730" width="10" height="100" rx="5" fill="#8FA689"/><text x="96" y="771" font-size="20" fill="#342E2A"><tspan font-weight="700">Kids at home?</tspan> Do not display toys or remotes where children</text><text x="96" y="805" font-size="20" fill="#342E2A">might be tempted to climb, and anchor every bookcase.</text><text x="60" y="872" font-size="15" fill="#6E645C">Anchoring and climbing tips: U.S. Consumer Product Safety Commission (CPSC). Styling formula: our styling advice.</text></svg><figcaption>Bookshelf styling formula: anchor first, zigzag the visual weight, style in groups of three, leave about a third empty and keep heavy items low.</figcaption></figure>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">The formula works for a tall bookcase, a built-in or a small ladder shelf. Once it clicks, all the bookshelf decor ideas below are just variations on it.</p>



