<h2 class="wp-block-heading">How to Style a Bookshelf in 6 Steps</h2>



<ol class="wp-block-list">
<li><strong>Anchor the bookcase.</strong> Use the anti-tip kit that came with it, or a furniture strap screwed into a wall stud.</li>


<li><strong>Empty every shelf.</strong> Dust the shelves and sort books into keep, donate and move-elsewhere piles.</li>


<li><strong>Place heavy books first.</strong> Big books and baskets go on the lowest shelves, where they also make the bookcase more stable.</li>


<li><strong>Add books in mixed directions.</strong> Upright rows on some shelves, flat stacks on others.</li>


<li><strong>Style with objects.</strong> Vases, bowls, a plant and a framed print in groups of three, zigzagging from shelf to shelf.</li>


<li><strong>Edit.</strong> Step back, remove one or two pieces and make sure every shelf has some empty space.</li>
</ol>



<h2 class="wp-block-heading">Book Styling Ideas</h2>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Books are the main decor on any bookshelf, so these bookshelf decorating ideas start with how you place them.</p>



<h3 class="wp-block-heading">1. Mix Upright Rows and Horizontal Stacks</h3>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">A shelf of books all standing in one direction reads as a wall. Break it up: stand six to ten books upright on one side and lay a short stack of three to five flat on the other side. The change of direction creates rhythm, and a flat stack also works as a natural bookend, so you need fewer store-bought ones.</p>



<h3 class="wp-block-heading">2. Arrange Books by Color</h3>



<figure class="wp-block-image size-full"><img decoding="async" width="1024" height="1024" src="https://dailyspacehacks.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/bookshelf-decor-02-books-by-color.jpg" alt="White bookshelf with books arranged by color from cream to sage green, terracotta and brown, with small ceramic pieces at the end of each row" class="wp-image-1740" srcset="https://dailyspacehacks.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/bookshelf-decor-02-books-by-color.jpg 1024w, https://dailyspacehacks.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/bookshelf-decor-02-books-by-color-300x300.jpg 300w, https://dailyspacehacks.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/bookshelf-decor-02-books-by-color-150x150.jpg 150w, https://dailyspacehacks.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/bookshelf-decor-02-books-by-color-768x768.jpg 768w" sizes="(max-width: 1024px) 100vw, 1024px" /></figure>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Grouping books by color, from cream to sage to terracotta to brown, gives a calm, gradual look. It suits people who remember their books by cover rather than by author. If you need to find titles quickly, color-block only one or two shelves and keep the rest in your usual order.</p>



<h3 class="wp-block-heading">3. Use Books as Risers</h3>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">A flat stack of two or three books under a small vase, bowl or candle lifts it to a better height and ties the decor to the books. It is the easiest free trick for making small objects look intentional.</p>



<h2 class="wp-block-heading">Object and Styling Ideas</h2>



<h3 class="wp-block-heading">4. Style in Groups of Three</h3>



<figure class="wp-block-image size-full"><img decoding="async" width="1024" height="1024" src="https://dailyspacehacks.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/bookshelf-decor-03-rule-of-three-shelf.jpg" alt="Oak shelf styled with a tall stoneware vase of dried flowers, a stack of three books topped with a wooden bowl and a small framed fern print" class="wp-image-1741" srcset="https://dailyspacehacks.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/bookshelf-decor-03-rule-of-three-shelf.jpg 1024w, https://dailyspacehacks.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/bookshelf-decor-03-rule-of-three-shelf-300x300.jpg 300w, https://dailyspacehacks.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/bookshelf-decor-03-rule-of-three-shelf-150x150.jpg 150w, https://dailyspacehacks.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/bookshelf-decor-03-rule-of-three-shelf-768x768.jpg 768w" sizes="(max-width: 1024px) 100vw, 1024px" /></figure>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Odd numbers look natural to the eye, and three is the easiest number to style. Pair something tall (a vase with stems), something medium (a book stack with a bowl on top) and something flat or low (a leaning framed print). Keep a little space between the three pieces so each one is visible.</p>



<h3 class="wp-block-heading">5. Zigzag the Visual Weight</h3>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">If one shelf has books on the left and objects on the right, flip it on the next shelf. Your eye moves across the bookcase in a zigzag instead of seeing heavy columns of books on one side. Repeat a color or material, such as terracotta, on two or three shelves to link the pattern.</p>



<h3 class="wp-block-heading">6. Leave About a Third of Each Shelf Empty</h3>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Empty space is what makes a shelf look styled rather than stored. Aim to leave roughly a third of each shelf open, and move the extra books to a closet, another room or the donate box. Our guide on <a href="https://dailyspacehacks.com/how-to-declutter-your-home/">how to declutter your home</a> helps you decide what to keep.</p>



<h3 class="wp-block-heading">7. Lean One Framed Print</h3>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">A small framed print leaning at the back of a shelf adds color and depth without a nail in the wall. Choose simple botanicals, landscapes or abstracts in your palette, and use one print every two or three shelves so the art does not compete with the books.</p>



<h3 class="wp-block-heading">8. Add Greenery</h3>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">A trailing plant at the top corner softens the straight lines of a bookcase. Pothos is a popular choice, but the ASPCA lists <a href="https://www.aspca.org/pet-care/animal-poison-control/toxic-and-non-toxic-plants/golden-pothos" target="_blank" rel="noopener">golden pothos</a> as toxic to cats and dogs, so keep it out of pets&#8217; reach or use a good faux trailing plant. Real plants need light, so put them on the shelf nearest a window.</p>



<h3 class="wp-block-heading">9. Mix Heights and Textures</h3>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Matte stoneware, glossy glazes, raw wood and woven fibers next to paper book spines give a shelf depth. Vary heights too: one tall piece per shelf, a few medium ones and something low. If every object is the same size, the shelf looks flat even when it is tidy.</p>



<h2 class="wp-block-heading">Storage and Small-Space Ideas</h2>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Some of the most useful bookshelf decor ideas are really storage ideas in disguise.</p>



<h3 class="wp-block-heading">10. Hide Clutter in Baskets on the Bottom Shelves</h3>



<figure class="wp-block-image size-full"><img loading="lazy" decoding="async" width="1024" height="1024" src="https://dailyspacehacks.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/bookshelf-decor-04-baskets-bottom-shelves.jpg" alt="Lower shelves of an oak bookshelf with lidded seagrass baskets and linen storage boxes below a row of books, above a cream rug" class="wp-image-1742" srcset="https://dailyspacehacks.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/bookshelf-decor-04-baskets-bottom-shelves.jpg 1024w, https://dailyspacehacks.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/bookshelf-decor-04-baskets-bottom-shelves-300x300.jpg 300w, https://dailyspacehacks.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/bookshelf-decor-04-baskets-bottom-shelves-150x150.jpg 150w, https://dailyspacehacks.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/bookshelf-decor-04-baskets-bottom-shelves-768x768.jpg 768w" sizes="auto, (max-width: 1024px) 100vw, 1024px" /></figure>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">The lowest shelves are perfect for closed storage: lidded seagrass baskets and linen boxes hide cables, games and paperwork and keep small hands from pulling things out. Matching containers look calm, and heavy baskets down low help the bookcase stay stable. For more ideas, see our <a href="https://dailyspacehacks.com/toy-storage-ideas/">toy storage ideas</a>.</p>



<h3 class="wp-block-heading">11. Use a Ladder Shelf in a Small Room</h3>



<figure class="wp-block-image size-full"><img loading="lazy" decoding="async" width="1024" height="1024" src="https://dailyspacehacks.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/bookshelf-decor-05-ladder-shelf-bedroom.jpg" alt="Slim oak ladder bookshelf in the corner of a small bedroom holding books, a small plant, a bowl and a woven basket next to a linen bed" class="wp-image-1743" srcset="https://dailyspacehacks.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/bookshelf-decor-05-ladder-shelf-bedroom.jpg 1024w, https://dailyspacehacks.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/bookshelf-decor-05-ladder-shelf-bedroom-300x300.jpg 300w, https://dailyspacehacks.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/bookshelf-decor-05-ladder-shelf-bedroom-150x150.jpg 150w, https://dailyspacehacks.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/bookshelf-decor-05-ladder-shelf-bedroom-768x768.jpg 768w" sizes="auto, (max-width: 1024px) 100vw, 1024px" /></figure>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">No space for a full bookcase? A slim ladder shelf leans into a corner and takes up very little floor space, so it works in a small bedroom or beside a desk. Its narrow top shelves hold light items, and the wider lower shelves hold books and a basket. Anchor it to the wall with the included hardware or an anti-tip kit. More ideas in our <a href="https://dailyspacehacks.com/small-bedroom-storage-ideas/">small bedroom storage ideas</a>.</p>



<h3 class="wp-block-heading">12. Paint or Paper the Back Panel</h3>



<figure class="wp-block-image size-full"><img loading="lazy" decoding="async" width="1024" height="1024" src="https://dailyspacehacks.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/bookshelf-decor-06-sage-back-panel.jpg" alt="White built-in bookshelf with a sage green painted back panel showing off cream pitchers, terracotta pots, bowls and stacked books" class="wp-image-1744" srcset="https://dailyspacehacks.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/bookshelf-decor-06-sage-back-panel.jpg 1024w, https://dailyspacehacks.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/bookshelf-decor-06-sage-back-panel-300x300.jpg 300w, https://dailyspacehacks.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/bookshelf-decor-06-sage-back-panel-150x150.jpg 150w, https://dailyspacehacks.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/bookshelf-decor-06-sage-back-panel-768x768.jpg 768w" sizes="auto, (max-width: 1024px) 100vw, 1024px" /></figure>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Painting the back of a bookcase in sage, clay or deep blue makes cream ceramics and light books stand out. Peel-and-stick wallpaper is a renter-friendly alternative, and on a flat-pack bookcase you can cut a thin board to size, paint it and slide it in.</p>



<h2 class="wp-block-heading">Lighting and Finishing Touches</h2>



<h3 class="wp-block-heading">13. Add Warm Shelf Lighting</h3>



<figure class="wp-block-image size-full"><img loading="lazy" decoding="async" width="1024" height="1024" src="https://dailyspacehacks.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/bookshelf-decor-07-led-shelf-lighting.jpg" alt="Oak bookshelf at dusk with warm LED strip lights under each shelf lighting books, vases and a small plant beside a cozy armchair with a knit throw" class="wp-image-1745" srcset="https://dailyspacehacks.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/bookshelf-decor-07-led-shelf-lighting.jpg 1024w, https://dailyspacehacks.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/bookshelf-decor-07-led-shelf-lighting-300x300.jpg 300w, https://dailyspacehacks.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/bookshelf-decor-07-led-shelf-lighting-150x150.jpg 150w, https://dailyspacehacks.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/bookshelf-decor-07-led-shelf-lighting-768x768.jpg 768w" sizes="auto, (max-width: 1024px) 100vw, 1024px" /></figure>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Battery or plug-in LED strip lights under each shelf turn a bookcase into a cozy evening glow. Pick a warm white color, hide the strip under the front edge of the shelf and run the cable down the back corner. Follow the product&#8217;s instructions and do not cover the light or power supply with books or fabric.</p>



<h3 class="wp-block-heading">14. Swap a Few Pieces With the Seasons</h3>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Keep the books and baskets the same and change just two or three objects: dried grasses and a small pumpkin in fall, a mini tree or greenery in winter, fresh stems in spring. It keeps the shelves feeling new without extra clutter. Our <a href="https://dailyspacehacks.com/coffee-table-decor/">coffee table decor ideas</a> use the same trick.</p>



<h3 class="wp-block-heading">15. Keep It Safe for Kids</h3>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">A styled bookcase still has to be safe. Anchor it, keep heavy books and baskets low, and do not display or store toys, remotes or other tempting items in places where kids might be tempted to climb up to reach them, as the CPSC advises. Keep glass vases out of children&#8217;s reach, or swap them for unbreakable pieces, and use flameless candles on bookshelves.</p>



<h2 class="wp-block-heading">Before and After: One Bookshelf, Two Looks</h2>



<figure class="wp-block-image size-full"><img loading="lazy" decoding="async" width="1024" height="1024" src="https://dailyspacehacks.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/bookshelf-decor-08-before-after.jpg" alt="Bookshelf before and after: shelves crammed with piled books, papers, cables and a box on the left; books grouped upright and in stacks with vases, a plant, a framed print and baskets on the right" class="wp-image-1746" srcset="https://dailyspacehacks.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/bookshelf-decor-08-before-after.jpg 1024w, https://dailyspacehacks.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/bookshelf-decor-08-before-after-300x300.jpg 300w, https://dailyspacehacks.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/bookshelf-decor-08-before-after-150x150.jpg 150w, https://dailyspacehacks.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/bookshelf-decor-08-before-after-768x768.jpg 768w" sizes="auto, (max-width: 1024px) 100vw, 1024px" /></figure>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">On the left, every shelf is packed with piled books, papers, cables and a box. On the right, the same bookcase holds fewer, better-grouped books, a few ceramics, one plant, one framed print and two baskets that hide the rest. Most of the change came from removing items and leaving space, which is why these bookshelf decor ideas cost little or nothing. For wall shelves elsewhere in the home, see our <a href="https://dailyspacehacks.com/diy-floating-shelves/">DIY floating shelves</a> and <a href="https://dailyspacehacks.com/kitchen-wall-decor-ideas/">kitchen wall decor ideas</a>.</p>



<h2 class="wp-block-heading">Common Bookshelf Decor Mistakes</h2>



<ul class="wp-block-list">
<li><strong>Skipping the anchor.</strong> A full bookcase is heavy and can tip; secure it before styling.</li>


<li><strong>Filling every inch.</strong> Without empty space, even pretty objects look like storage.</li>


<li><strong>All books in one direction.</strong> Mix upright rows and flat stacks.</li>


<li><strong>Tiny objects scattered everywhere.</strong> Group small items or swap them for fewer, larger pieces.</li>


<li><strong>Heavy items up high.</strong> Keep big books and baskets on the lower shelves.</li>


<li><strong>Too many colors.</strong> Repeat two or three colors across the shelves.</li>
</ul>



<h2 class="wp-block-heading">Bookshelf Decor FAQ</h2>



<h3 class="wp-block-heading">How do you decorate a bookshelf without it looking cluttered?</h3>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Leave about a third of each shelf empty, group objects in threes, mix upright books with flat stacks and hide small items in baskets on the bottom shelves.</p>



<h3 class="wp-block-heading">How many books should go on each shelf?</h3>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">There is no fixed number. A good guide is one upright group and one flat stack or object group per shelf, with some empty space left over.</p>



<h3 class="wp-block-heading">What can I put on a bookshelf besides books?</h3>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Vases, bowls, a small plant, a framed print, a flameless candle, baskets and one or two personal pieces like travel finds.</p>



<h3 class="wp-block-heading">Should a bookshelf be anchored to the wall?</h3>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Yes. The CPSC advises anchoring bookcases and dressers securely to the wall in any home where children live or visit; an average of 12,500 children are treated in emergency rooms for tip-over injuries each year.</p>



<h2 class="wp-block-heading">Final Thoughts</h2>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">The best bookshelf decor ideas are simple: anchor the bookcase, mix how your books stand, style in threes, zigzag the weight, leave breathing room and keep heavy things low. Start with what you own, add one plant and one print, and your shelves will look styled in an afternoon. For more small-space ideas, see our <a href="https://dailyspacehacks.com/storage-ideas-for-small-spaces/">storage ideas for small spaces</a>.</p>



